{"genre": "Reuters", "passage_A": "The Building Societies Association (BSA) is contemplating taking the matter of draft takeover protection legislation to the European Court of Justice. They believe this move is necessary due to the potential for the legislation to cause significant financial hardship for thousands of small British firms and their shareholders. The proposed legislation, part of the Financial Services Bill under a coalition government, allows UK companies to defer tax payments when acquiring a controlling stake in another firm.

BSA has expressed concern that this could force many companies to divert substantial funds to such acquisitions, potentially leading to dire financial situations and even bankruptcy. The association contends that the legislation violates EU merger regulations, introducing substantial legal uncertainties and heightening political risks for the UK.

Moreover, the BSA argues that the UK might breach cross-border acquisition laws if the current stance on the legislation remains unchanged.", "passage_B": "The Building Societies Association is considering taking the issue of the draft of the takeover protection law to the European Court of Justice. The BSA believes that the law threatens to put thousands of small British companies and their shareholders in great financial difficulty. The BSA has warned that the law could lead to thousands of companies having to pay large sums of money to acquire a controlling stake in their business, which could lead to difficult financial circumstances and even bankruptcy. The BSA has also warned that the law violates the European Union's merger regulations, which creates major legal uncertainty and political risk for the United Kingdom."}

